    "data": {
      "title": "Balance seesaw and center of mass",
      "topics": [],
      "fastest": true,
      "reverse": false,
      "shortest": true,
      "solution": "const N = Number(readline());\nlet massCount = 0;\nlet columnSum = 0;\n\nfor (let row = 0; row < N - 1; row++) {\n    const line = readline();\n    for (let column = 0; column < line.length; column++) {\n        if (line[column] === '#') {\n            massCount++;\n            columnSum += column;\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n\nconst fulcrum = readline().indexOf('X');\n// The center of mass is columnSum / massCount.\n// Balance requires its distance from the support center to be at most 0.5:\n// abs(columnSum / massCount - fulcrum) <= 0.5.\n// Multiply both sides by 2 * massCount to compare integers exactly.\nconst balanced = Math.abs(2 * columnSum - 2 * fulcrum * massCount) <= massCount;\nconsole.log(balanced ? 'In balance' : 'Not in balance');",
      "statement": "Read the ASCII art of a seesaw and determine whether it is in balance.\n\nThe drawing shows blocks stacked on a continuous surface. Each {{#}} represents an equal mass at the center of its column. Spaces have no mass. The last line contains the support, marked by {{X}}. The support does not contribute mass. The {{#}} characters in the surface do contribute mass.\n\nColumns are numbered from {{0}} on the left. The horizontal <<center of mass>> is the average column number of all {{#}} characters. Vertical positions do not affect this value.\n\nThe support is centered on the column of {{X}} and is one column wide: it extends half a column to each side. The seesaw is in balance when its center of mass is inside this interval, <<including both edges>>. Do not round or truncate the center of mass.\n\nPrint {{In balance}} if the seesaw is in balance. Otherwise, print {{Not in balance}}.",
      "testCases": [
